y:947`, `parse()` defaults to `case_sensitive=True`. Its own docstring says \"The default behaviour is to match strings case insensitively\", and `search`, `findall`, `compile` and `Parser` all default to `False`. So `parse.parse(\"Hello {}\", \"hELLO world\")` returns `None`, while `compile(\"Hello {}\").parse(\"hELLO world\")` matches. These two tests catch it:\n- `TestCaseSensitivity::test_parse_case_insensitive_by_default`\n- `TestCaseSensitivity::test_parse_default_matches_compile_default`\n\nChanging that default to `case_sensitive=False` should make the whole suite pass.\n\n**What the suite covers:**\n- Fixed, named and positional fields, `{{ }}` escapes, and regex special characters in plain text\n- Exact matching with `parse`, including rejecting a trailing newline\n- Every number type, including base detection, grouping, width, sign, fill and alignment\n- Text types, width and precision limits, alignment, and unusual fill characters, plus `extract_format` and invalid format strings\n- Field names with dots, brackets or hyphens, names that would clash, and names repeated in one format\n- Match positions, `search` and `findall` with start and end positions, and returning raw matches instead of results\n- Custom types, `with_pattern` (including patterns with their own groups, which tests that later fields still line up), every built-in date/time type, and `strftime`-style formats\n- Time zones (`FixedTzOffset`), pickling a compiled parser, and formats with many fields\n\nSeven of my first-draft tests also failed, but those were my own wrong expectations, not library bugs, and I corrected them. Two are worth knowing about: `{:s}` matches whitespace, not any string, and for plain text fields the width is a minimum length that includes the padding.\n\nThe run also prints one deprecation warning from Python's `strptime` when a date has no year. It comes from the year-not-specified test and doesn't affect the results.","ttft_ms":2665,"type":"result","duration_ms":